A||||19||daved||Steering Rack: Is Chevette an alternative source?||||1||
Z||000000||daved||01-20-2000||10:57 PM||||My 86GT steering rack bushings are badly worn. I can get my brother to machine new bronze bushings, but it would take a couple of weeks of back and forth for an exact fit.<BR> My car is a daily driver, and I cant do without my daily "fix". <BR>My question is; does anyone know if the Chevette rack is a good fit? <BR> I would guess it would not come with the boss for the steering damper, which would be no loss for a couple of weeks.<BR>  Any other rack that might fit?<BR>Thanks,  Dave||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||01-20-2000||11:28 PM||||I think one from that plastic mid engine thing pontiac built back in the late 80'2 will fit||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||01-20-2000||11:40 PM||||LOL||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||01-20-2000||11:43 PM||||I'm beginning to suspect the rack bushings are shot in my 85 GT too. What did your car do? Does the front end rattle over bumps and feel like something's loose and banging around? I've replace the steering stabilizer, all the tie rod ends, and front wheel bearings. The ball joints and shocks checked out ok. That pretty much leaves the rack, right? ||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||01-21-2000||12:11 AM||||Make sure its not the tie rods. If you take them off of the knuckle and they simply fall down then they have too much clearence. If they stay in place then they are okay. I had that happen in my 87. I had the car wandering to the right even if I had the wheel steady and that wheel could be turned ever so slightly when it was on the jack. If its the inner tie rod than it can be replaced without taking the rack out. Better than taking out the whole rack!!!<P>It would not be the part that is bolted to the knuckle it would be the part coming out of the rack that would be bad. This can be changed out!<P>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-21-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 01-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||01-21-2000||06:35 AM||||Rodney Dickman makes some of the bushings for the racks.||||reg||1
Z||000007||pollock||01-21-2000||09:38 AM||||Reman rack:  $100 from Autozone.  Lifetime warranty.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||deceler8||01-21-2000||10:01 AM||||But does a Chevette rack fit ?<P>It's a valid question...c'mon, Chevettes are never driven like race cars...if the racks fit, would be a shame if the salvage yards are full of pristine ones that can be had for nothing...||||reg||5
Z||000009||Raydar||01-21-2000||12:16 PM||||And expanding on the previous... Is there another rack that will fit an '88? It's different from the earlier cars, and it's not available from the parts stores. At any price. (Although the Fiero Store has them. They just want your first-born male child, as payment.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||01-21-2000||12:33 PM||||Deceler8,<P>Go to a auto parts place during a non-peak hour and they will be happy to let you know if they will fit.  First look up the rack (GM) number in your 22p if you have one, otherwise ask them what rack your Fiero would take.  Then ask them what rack the Chevettes take.  I think that I remember something about the steering ratio was different since most Chevettes had power steering so it might physically fit, but have the incorrect ratio.  Then you will know if the #'s are the same and can head to the bone yard to pull a Chevette rack.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000011||batboy||01-21-2000||03:24 PM||||I called a local salvage yard, they looked up in their parts exchange book and found that the only rack listed that will fit a Fiero is a Fiero rack.||||reg||1
Z||000012||lowCG||01-21-2000||04:36 PM||||Hey Batboy,try jiggling the tierod up and down to see if the same rattling sound happens.My old car had this problem,bad bushings inside the rack.<BR>I have an old  German Opel rack that needed different tierods in order to work,much better metals/manufacturing quality.Probably more hassle than anyone else would be willing to endure to install,however.||||reg||1
Z||000013||DRH||01-21-2000||05:10 PM||||Batboy, My 88 Formula would have fit your description to a T. It was the passenger side rack bushing (plastic). Pre 88s are a different design but probably similar. Bushing was $18 from Pontiac (still plastic)... Later saw that Rodney Dickman  has metal ones for about the same... also has 84-87: <A HREF="http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/8487rackbushing.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.execpc.com/~rodney1/8487rackbushing.htm</A> <P>The rack is not that hard to disassemble and reassemble (not sure about availability of other parts if needed). About the only thing not fairly obvious was setting the alignment and preload of the rack and pinion. Pontiac Service Manual tells you everything you need to know if you have or can get one. <P>Oops, Daved looks like we took over your post. I don't know about the Chevette. Rodney's bushing is aluminum. You might want to check the link above as an alternative to brass..<p>[This message has been edited by DRH (edited 01-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000014||terryk||01-22-2000||02:17 AM||||Is the 85 GT rack different than the 86SE V6 rack?||||reg||1
Z||000015||DRH||01-22-2000||03:34 AM||||The Fiero Store lists different part numbers as follows:<P>84-87 4 cyl<BR>85 6 cyl<BR>86-87 6 cyl<BR>88 all<P>Looks like 85 GT and 86 SE 6 would be different.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||01-22-2000||03:39 AM||||Why would a steering rack for a 6 be any different from one for a 4?  It's the same car, right?  Can somebody clue me in please?!||||reg||1
Z||000017||batboy||01-22-2000||11:56 AM||||My guess is that the steering ratio is slightly different, I'm assuming they still interchange ok, except maybe the 88, that might be an oddball. Didn't the GT's have a closer ratio?||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||01-22-2000||04:23 PM||||I did a little scouting around at the local Autozone this am.  According to their computer, all Fiero racks fit all Fieros.  They appppparently make 2 different racks, though.  One is called the "Sport" steering and the other is um...not.  One of them (I forget which) is 3 turns lock-to-lock (requires less effort to turn according to Autozone) and the other is 3.5 turns.  They checked the part number against about 30 other cars and nothing was the same.  I guess Tom was correct in his 1st post.  You have to buy one from "that plastic mid engine thing Pontiac built back in the late 80's".  BTW--The rack costs $115 at my Autozone with a $50 core charge.  Here are the Autozone part #s in case anyone's interested:  Sport steering-(3.0 turns)-Atsco 6813-----Not sport steering-(3.5 turns)-Atsco 6814  ||||reg||1
Z||000019||cobra427||01-24-2000||02:01 AM||||The 22P book says that for the 84-87's steering rack ass.,there were only two used.<BR>It list as follows:<BR>1984 exc.WS6.......pt#7846406<BR>1984 w/WS6.........pt#7847087<BR>1985...............pt#7847087<BR>1986-87 PM37(coups)pt#7846406<BR>1986-87 PG97 (GT's)pt#7847087<BR>No rack listing for the 88's as they are listed as separate pieces||||reg||1
Z||000020||fieroc4||01-24-2000||02:22 AM||||Monkeyman said "One of them (I forget which) is 3 turns lock-to-lock (requires less effort to turn according to Autozone) and the other is 3.5 turns."<P>I'd hate to dissagree with an "AutoZone Expert" but logic tells me that 3.5:1 is easier to turn than 3.0:1.  If the tires turn 45° in each direction a 3:1 means you are giving 30° of turn for each turn of the wheel where with a 3.5:1 you would only be moving the tires 25.71° with each turn.  But that's just my 2¢.<P>Dan||||reg||3
